<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext>
  <article xml:id="lin-alg-2-day-2">
    <title>Basic Math</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-review">
      <title>Review</title>

      <ul>
        <li><p>What does it mean for a set of vectors to be <em>linearly independent</em>?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does it mean for a set of vectors to <em>span</em> a vector space?</p></li>
      </ul>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-independent-extension">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>\mathbf{v}_1,\dots,\mathbf{v}_k\in V</m>, and suppose they are linearly independent.
            Let <m>\mathbf{u}\in V</m>. Then
            <m>\mathbf{v}_1,\dots,\mathbf{v}_k,\mathbf{u}</m> are linearly independent if and only if
            <m>\mathbf{u}\notin\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{v}_1,\dots,\mathbf{v}_k)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-bases">
      <title>Bases</title>

      <p>
        A <term>basis</term> of a vector space <m>V</m> is a subset of <m>V</m> that is both linearly independent and spanning.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-finite-spanning-set-has-basis">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Any vector space <m>V</m> of the form <m>\operatorname{span}(S)</m>, where <m>S</m> is finite, has a basis.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Choose a linearly independent subset <m>I\subseteq S</m> of maximal cardinality; this is possible because <m>S</m> is finite.
            If <m>I=S</m>, then <m>I</m> is both spanning and linearly independent, so it is a basis.
          </p>
          <p>
            Otherwise, let <m>\mathbf{s}\in S\setminus I</m>. Maximality of <m>I</m> implies that
            <m>I\cup\{\mathbf{s}\}</m> is linearly dependent. By <xref ref="ex-independent-extension"/>,
            <m>\mathbf{s}\in\operatorname{span}(I)</m>. Thus every element of <m>S</m> lies in
            <m>\operatorname{span}(I)</m>, so
          </p>
          <me>
            V=\operatorname{span}(S)\subseteq\operatorname{span}(I)\subseteq V.
          </me>
          <p>
            Hence <m>\operatorname{span}(I)=V</m>, and <m>I</m> is a basis.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-where-finiteness">
        <statement>
          <p>Which step used finiteness?</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        A vector space that can be written as the span of finitely many vectors is called <term>finite-dimensional</term>.
        We call the size of a basis the <term>dimension</term> of a vector space. By the previous result, every finite-dimensional
        vector space has a finite basis, which is where the term comes from. However, this definition could hypothetically fail
        to make sense: if a vector space had one basis of size <m>2</m> and another of size <m>3</m>, what would its dimension be?
        Luckily, we can prove this cannot happen.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-base-size">
        <statement>
          <p>Every basis of a finite-dimensional vector space has the same finite size.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        To prove this, we will use a famous lemma that gives a way to swap elements of bases.
      </p>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-steinitz-exchange">
        <title>Steinitz Exchange Lemma</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> be two bases of a finite-dimensional vector space. For any
            <m>\mathbf{v}\in B_2\setminus B_1</m>, there exists <m>\mathbf{u}\in B_1\setminus B_2</m> such that
            <m>(B_1\setminus\{\mathbf{u}\})\cup\{\mathbf{v}\}</m> is a basis.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Let <m>\mathbf{v}\in B_2\setminus B_1</m>. Since <m>B_1</m> is a basis, there are distinct vectors
            <m>\mathbf{u}_1,\dots,\mathbf{u}_n\in B_1</m> and scalars <m>a_1,\dots,a_n</m>, not all zero, such that
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i\mathbf{u}_i.
          </me>
          <p>
            Some <m>\mathbf{u}_i</m> with <m>a_i\neq 0</m> must lie in <m>B_1\setminus B_2</m>. Otherwise every
            <m>\mathbf{u}_i</m> with nonzero coefficient would lie in <m>B_2</m>; because
            <m>\mathbf{v}\notin B_1</m>, the vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is distinct from all of these <m>\mathbf{u}_i</m>,
            and the displayed equation would give a nontrivial linear dependence among elements of <m>B_2</m>.
            Relabel if necessary so that <m>a_n\neq 0</m> and <m>\mathbf{u}_n\in B_1\setminus B_2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            We claim that <m>(B_1\setminus\{\mathbf{u}_n\})\cup\{\mathbf{v}\}</m> is a basis. First, it is linearly independent.
            Indeed, <m>\mathbf{v}\notin\operatorname{span}(B_1\setminus\{\mathbf{u}_n\})</m>: if it were, then
            <m>\mathbf{v}</m> would have an expression in the basis <m>B_1</m> with coefficient <m>0</m> on
            <m>\mathbf{u}_n</m>, contradicting uniqueness of basis coordinates since the displayed expression has
            <m>a_n\neq 0</m>. By <xref ref="ex-independent-extension"/>, replacing <m>\mathbf{u}_n</m> by
            <m>\mathbf{v}</m> therefore preserves linear independence.
          </p>
          <p>
            To show spanning, solve the displayed equation for <m>\mathbf{u}_n</m>:
          </p>
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_n=\frac{1}{a_n}\left(\mathbf{v}-\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}a_i\mathbf{u}_i\right).
          </me>
          <p>
            Thus <m>\mathbf{u}_n\in\operatorname{span}((B_1\setminus\{\mathbf{u}_n\})\cup\{\mathbf{v}\})</m>.
            Every other element of <m>B_1</m> is already in the replacement set, so that set spans <m>B_1</m>, and hence spans
            <m>V</m>. Therefore <m>(B_1\setminus\{\mathbf{u}_n\})\cup\{\mathbf{v}\}</m> is a basis.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        Using the exchange lemma, we can prove <xref ref="thm-base-size"/>.
      </p>

      <proof>
        <title>Proof of the basis-size theorem</title>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="thm-finite-spanning-set-has-basis"/>, choose a finite basis
          <m>B=\{\mathbf{b}_1,\dots,\mathbf{b}_n\}</m> of <m>V</m>. Let <m>C</m> be any other basis.
          We first show that <m>C</m> has at most <m>n</m> elements.
        </p>
        <p>
          Suppose instead that <m>C</m> contains <m>n+1</m> distinct vectors
          <m>\mathbf{c}_1,\dots,\mathbf{c}_{n+1}</m>. Starting from <m>B</m>, process these vectors one at a time.
          If <m>\mathbf{c}_j</m> is already in the current basis, make no change. If it is not, apply
          <xref ref="lem-steinitz-exchange"/> to exchange <m>\mathbf{c}_j</m> into the current basis while removing
          an element not in <m>C</m>. Consequently, none of the previously inserted vectors
          <m>\mathbf{c}_1,\dots,\mathbf{c}_{j-1}</m> is removed.
        </p>
        <p>
          After processing all <m>n+1</m> vectors, we would have a basis with exactly <m>n</m> elements that contains the
          <m>n+1</m> distinct vectors <m>\mathbf{c}_1,\dots,\mathbf{c}_{n+1}</m>, which is impossible. Hence
          <m>|C|\leq n</m>. In particular, <m>C</m> is finite. Applying the same argument with <m>C</m> as the finite basis
          and <m>B</m> as the other basis gives <m>n\leq |C|</m>. Therefore <m>|C|=n</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-exercises">
      <title>Exercises</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-steinitz-example">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Apply <xref ref="lem-steinitz-exchange"/> to
            <m>B_1=\{(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)\}</m> and
            <m>B_2=\{(1,0,0),(1,1,0),(1,1,1)\}</m> with <m>\mathbf{v}=(1,1,0)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-spanning-contains-basis">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that a set of vectors is spanning in a finite-dimensional vector space if and only if it contains a basis.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-independent-contained-basis">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Show that a set of vectors is linearly independent in a finite-dimensional vector space if and only if it is contained in a basis.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        Some properties of bases break down if we use integer coefficients instead of coefficients from a field such as
        <m>\mathbb{R}</m>. Let <m>\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{Z}}(S)</m> denote the set of integer linear combinations
        of elements of <m>S</m>. We call a set of vectors <term>integer-linearly independent</term> if the only integer
        linear combination of them that equals <m>0</m> is the trivial combination, in which all coefficients are zero.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-integer-span-counterexample">
        <introduction>
          <p>Let <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> denote the integers.</p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <p>
            Explain why <m>\mathbb{Z}\neq\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{2\}</m> and
            <m>\mathbb{Z}\neq\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{3\}</m>.
          </p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Show that <m>\mathbb{Z}=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{2,3\}</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <p>Show that a nontrivial integer linear combination of <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> equals <m>0</m>.</p>
        </task>
        <conclusion>
          <p>
            Conclude that although <m>\{2,3\}</m> spans <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> over <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>, it contains no subset
            that both spans <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> over <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> and is integer-linearly independent.
          </p>
        </conclusion>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
